PostgreSQL 18.7 added attribute versioning. To stay backpatchable, it stores
the version of each attribute in pg_attribute.attoptions under the reserved
key "pg_attver", which users cannot set.

pgAdmin reads attoptions and presents them as editable column Variables, so
columns of tables and foreign tables show up with a bogus, non-settable
"pg_attver" option, and the reverse engineered SQL contains a
SET (pg_attver=...) clause. Drop the key when parsing the options, as
PostgreSQL itself does for spuriously supplied values.

See https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/release-18-7.html and
src/backend/catalog/README.attver.md in the PostgreSQL sources.

--- a/web/pgadmin/browser/server_groups/servers/databases/schemas/tables/columns/utils.py
+++ b/web/pgadmin/browser/server_groups/servers/databases/schemas/tables/columns/utils.py
@@ -648,15 +648,22 @@
             data['attprecision'] = None
 
     return data
+
+
+# Reserved key used internally by PostgreSQL 18.7+ for attribute
+# versioning, see src/backend/catalog/README.attver.md
+ATTRIBUTE_VERSION_OPTION = 'pg_attver'
 
 
 def parse_column_variables(col_variables):
     # We need to format variables according to client js collection
     spcoptions = []
     if col_variables is not None:
         for spcoption in col_variables:
             k, v = spcoption.split('=')
+            if k == ATTRIBUTE_VERSION_OPTION:
+                continue
             spcoptions.append({'name': k, 'value': v})
     return spcoptions
 
 
